Automatically calculated GIC can be remitted at the individual transaction level or for some account types by using a GIC remission form, which facilitates remission for multiple transactions or periods.

An account level remission form is available for:

income tax, and

FBT accounts.

A period level remission form is available for Super Guarantee (SG) Employer accounts for:

SG Shortfall GIC, or

SG Part 7 GIC.

When a percentage remission amount is entered in the form, the resulting remission amount is calculated by multiplying the total amount of GIC available by the percentage remission amount.

When the form is used to remit GIC, the total remission amount is broken down into individual postings.

The order in which GIC remission transactions are applied to account periods for the account level remission form is:

earliest to latest account periods for unpaid GIC, then

latest to earliest account periods for paid GIC.

Task 1 - Check non-pursuit/release transactions

Check the account for GIC subject to non-pursuit/release before offering or processing a GIC remission.

Pre-conversion non-pursuit/release transactions can be found in the:

legacy system, or

Income tax former account role.

Check Notes to see how much GIC has been released and calculate accordingly. Post-conversion non-pursuit/release transactions are in the GIC role.

If the account has previously been non-pursued as uneconomical to pursue, consider if the non-pursued debt should be re-raised prior to processing GIC remission.

Warning
Do not remit the amount of GIC subject to non-pursuit/release. If the GIC non-pursuit has been reduced via remission or credit amendment, the non-pursuit must be re-raised to absorb the GIC credits.
Task 2 - Complete account preparation

Complete account preparation before remitting GIC by:

resolving any outstanding suspense items to ensure all forms that impact on the GIC calculation have been finalised
resolving any outstanding review items with financial impact
updating GIC to the current date.

This will ensure all transactions are accounted for as part of your action.

