Reporting an incorrect refund

How to report and initiate recovery of incorrect refunds.

Last published 26 November 2024

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides the procedure on how to report and initiate recovery of incorrect refunds.

Incorrect refunds and credits pose a significant risk to revenue and the integrity of the taxpayer account. It is important that any identified incorrect refunds are actioned immediately and are also reported. This ensures that the Australian Taxation Office (ATO):

formal reporting obligations are met

accounts are corrected appropriately and

opportunities are identified to prevent these situations from occurring in the future.

All identified incorrect refunds must be:

actioned immediately to prevent the incorrect refund from issuing, or to recover the monies as soon as is practicable if they have issued

managed by the appropriate business area to ensure corrective action, adjustment, remediation and mitigation activities are undertaken

reported by the area that identified the incorrect refund using the appropriate reporting method, regardless of the value.

A refund which has issued by Electronic Funds Transfer (EFT) to an incorrect bank account is treated as an incorrect refund only in circumstances where:

the ATO has processed the bank account details incorrectly (ATO administrative error)

the error with the bank account details was due to the taxpayer or tax agent and the refund has not been recovered/retrieved from the wrong bank account first, before paying the refund to the correct bank account. 

