Reporting suspected external fraud

Outlines when and how to report suspected external fraud

Last published 26 February 2026

Favourite Notes Expand all 
Your reporting responsibilities

All employees and contracted individuals in the Australian Taxation Office (ATO), Australian Charities and Not-for-profits Commission (ACNC) and Tax Practitioners Board (TPB) have a responsibility to report suspicions and allegations of external fraud.

You do not need to know the identity of the fraudster to make a report.

Discuss your suspicion of fraud with your manager before making a report.

Contact your External fraud representative 
Opens in a new window
if there is doubt about whether your suspicion of external fraud is correct.

All reporting responsibilities for ATO, ACNC and TPB staff are summarised and detailed in the table below under How to report your suspicions and allegations of external fraud.

ACNC staff must report suspicions and allegations of external fraud in relation to the tax, superannuation and registry systems to the ATO, and report suspected external fraud in relation to the charity registration and regulatory systems to the ACNC.

TPB staff must report suspicions and allegations of external fraud in relation to the tax, superannuation and registry systems to the ATO, and report suspected external fraud in relation to the tax practitioner registration and regulatory systems to the TPB.

ATO staff must report suspected external fraud to Fraud and Criminal Behaviours (FCB) following endorsed processes in the summary table below. This includes reporting all 75% administrative shortfall penalties when imposed and 50% penalty decisions where fraud is suspected by submitting a Siebel Work Management Activity.

ATO staff must report suspected external fraud committed by registered tax agents, BAS agents or unregistered preparers to FCB and Individuals and Intermediaries
Opens in a new window
 simultaneously to ensure consideration of a referral to the TPB.

When making a report, all ATO and TPB staff should report allegations of external fraud, made to you by members of the community or identified by you when you are not at work to FCB via the Tax Integrity Centre (TIC)
Opens in a new window
. You must use the internal ATO Tip-Off form
Opens in a new window
 (community ATO Tip-Off form
Opens in a new window
 for TPB Board members) to ensure you comply with the Tax Whistleblower Protection Regime CEI
Opens in a new window
.

ACNC staff must report all suspicions and allegations of external fraud to their External fraud representative
Opens in a new window
.

The guide to a comprehensive fraud referral
Opens in a new window
 provides assistance to staff to identify the behaviour, evidence and intent that characterise referrals for criminal investigation.

The External fraud Chief Executive Instruction (CEI
Opens in a new window
) sets out your responsibilities for preventing, detecting and responding to suspected external fraud.

If you suspect any other serious crime, contact FCB IAC Triage Centre.
 

Identifying external fraud behaviours

External fraud comes from external parties dishonestly obtaining (including attempting to obtain) a gain or benefit, or causing a loss or risk of loss, by deception or other means against the tax, superannuation and registry systems, the tax practitioner or charity registration and regulatory systems. A benefit includes information. External parties can include taxpayers, their representatives (including tax and BAS agents) and third parties unknown to the taxpayer (including organised crime groups).

The table outlines examples of external fraud activities with the associated behaviour and driver. Information regarding the type of alleged behaviour and drivers will be requested when you report suspicion of external fraud.

Alleged Behaviour

	

This is how fraudsters deliberately exploit any of the four pillars of compliance (registration, lodgment, correct reporting and payment) to obtain a fraudulent benefit.

e.g., claiming GST credits for goods or services where GST was not included in the price; claiming tax refunds by using stolen identities; falsely applying for an ABN or charity registration with the ACNC.




Behavioural Driver

	

The behavioural driver describes the likely intended outcome that is driving the alleged behaviour. (e.g., financial gain of GST/IT refund fraud, illegal phoenix activities, identity crime, offshore tax evasion).

 

External Fraud Activity	Alleged Behaviour	Behavioural Driver
Providing false information to obtain an ABN and GST registration to commit BAS refund fraud	Dishonest registration	GST refund fraud
Operating cash-in-hand services to avoid payment of IT, GST, PAYGW and SG	Avoiding payment	Cash-in-hand
An employer inflating PAYGW to help the employee get a better refund at tax time.	Overclaiming	Refund fraud
Claiming GST credits for goods or services that were either never purchased, or where GST was not included in the price	Overclaiming	GST refund fraud
Reporting false single touch payroll data to enable IT (PAYGW) refund fraud	Under reporting	Inflated IT refund fraud
Deliberately setting up corporate vehicles to avoid paying PAYGW, GST and income tax	Avoiding payment	Financial gain
Avoiding debts by carrying out illegal phoenix activities 	Avoiding payment	Phoenix
Unauthorised changes on a taxpayer’s account by a second or third party. (e.g., changing bank account information so refund goes to fraudster)	Stealing information	Identity crime enabled fraud
Use false or stolen identities to claim tax refunds 	Stealing information	Identity crime enabled fraud
Deliberately overclaiming expenses	Overclaiming	Inflated IT refund fraud
Use offshore structures and arrangements to deliberately conceal assets or income	Under reporting	Offshore tax evasion
Unauthorised use of myGov to transfer super from APRA fund to SMSF established by fraudster	Stealing information	Financial gain

