Replacement Refunds - action re-crediting in ICP

Procedure to action replacement cheque requests in ICP.

Last published 15 May 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This procedure is used by staff across Frontline Operations including Superannuation teams. The procedure describes how to action account re-crediting requests in ICP. To escalate a request for a replacement refunds, go to Request for a replacement refund (via cheque or EFT).
Opens in a new window

Information

Separation of duties does:

not apply to operatives actioning credit balance request refund initiation or replacement work
apply to operatives actioning Superannuation Unclaimed Super Money work.

After a replacement refund is requested via a Siebel activity, ATO staff with access will:

stop the cheque in RBAnet,
Opens in a new window
 and
manually modify the disbursement status in ICP to Stop request sent.

Overnight, the accepted stop cheque information returned from the transactional banker in the DART file will:

automatically update the disbursement status to Stop confirmed, and 
cancel the refund against the relevant assessment so that the credit is available for re-issue.

Warning alert:
Do not action without RBA access.

Failure to perform required actions in tandem in all systems causes errors which require extensive ATO remediation. This will delay the subsequent refund.

Wound up self-managed super funds (SMSF)

The ATO is the administrative registrar for SMSFs and holds responsibility for ensuring the completion of administrative actions required for the fund to be considered wound up.

A wound-up SMSF should keep their bank account open until all credits are successfully paid out.

A wound-up SMSF is likely identifiable by:

an active relevant suppression in ICP
no active fund members in Client links in ICP
a finalised ‘Wind up’ activity in Siebel, or
a 'Super Fund Wound-Up Date' in Additional client details in ICP.

Note: The fund may put a wind-up date within their final annual return, but this is their desired date only. The fund is considered active until the wind-up actions are completed in full.

If unsure whether a SMSF is wound up, seek support from Frontline Services Support. Refer to Wind-up a self-managed super fund
Opens in a new window
 for more information.

Requests for re-issuing to non-Australian bank accounts

This procedure can be used to re-issue refunds initially issued as cheques to international bank accounts when no Australian bank account is available.

A manual refund escalation will then be required after a credit suppression is applied to the relevant account to retain the re-credited funds.

Ensure you familiarise yourself with Telegraphic Transfer process
Opens in a new window
 as a completed escalation template will be required after the refund is re-credited.

Taxpayers with open or pending CISC activities

If you identify an open or pending CISC Siebel escalation on the taxpayer, do not take any action where the escalation relates to your request, e.g. the lodgment being questioned.

Via service request connect your activity to the pending CISC activity. Update your activity with the relevant details in your Replacement Refund activity and finalise.

If the replacement relates to a period not being investigated by the CISC escalation, discuss with Frontline Services Support.

New reducing use of cheques legislation
Warning

As of January 2025, under section 8AAZLGC of the Taxation Administration Act 1953, the ATO started holding some refunds from issuing via cheque for up to 90 days while we ask for bank details.

Taxpayers are encouraged to provide bank details to avoid delays. Re-issuing actions should not be delayed pending taxpayer contact where details have not been provided.

If a DISB NO FID non-RBA review item is holding the refund, resolve it in accordance with Review item - auto-correctible review items.
Opens in a new window

