Update penalty and interest in ICP

Outlines the process to update penalties and interest in ICP

Last published 15 September 2025

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page outlines the process to update penalties and interest in ICP.

Recent updates

15/09/2025

	

Update to task 2.

	SD-97432
Procedure Steps
Before you begin

This procedure begins after you have identified an update of automated penalties or interest is required.

Check that the transactions on the account look correct. Issuing incorrect refunds poses a significant risk and undermines the integrity of the tax system.

To report any identified incorrect credits or refunds, refer to Reporting an incorrect credit/refund.
Opens in a new window

Warning

Follow the above to reduce the risk of incorrect credit/refund.

Penalty and interest process

The update penalty and interest process is used to bring automated penalties and interest on a taxpayer's accounts up to date. The process can be initiated at various account levels, with the options to update penalty and interest, penalty only, or interest only.

If selected at the Client level all accounts for the taxpayer will be subject to the update. Presently, this process includes the following penalties and interest types:

Interest on early payments
Non-electronic notification penalty
HECS indexation
ICL/SSL/AASL indexation
Superannuation Holding Account special account (SHAsa) credit interest
general interest charge (GIC).
Warning

The functionality to support calculation of the relevant penalty or interest may not be available from the commencement of the release.

You may need to manually calculate these amounts using support systems outside ICP (for example, TAP products).

After update is completed, it is important any periods now in credit are left overnight and not transferred out to allow the system to confirm their validity. The system will:

offset the credited period to a debit, or
identify the credit is potentially invalid and generate a Review Item - Invalid Credit (ESCP1185)
Opens in a new window
 for actioning.
Automated penalties and interest

Automated penalties and interest are calculated and applied based on the business rules specified for each penalty and interest type. These rules cover factors such as when a taxpayer should be subject to the penalty or interest, exclusions, methods of calculation, and automatic remission thresholds.

Penalties can be updated at assessment level, but GIC can only be updated at the role level (except for certain superannuation products).

Other types of penalties, judgment interest, cost and fines must be manually applied to taxpayer accounts.

Update penalty and interest action should be taken where it is necessary prior to doing a remission, or the balance of an account needs to match an amount provided in certain correspondence and legal documents. For example:

statement of account

notice under section 260-5 of the TAA 1953 (garnishee)

statutory demand (s459E)

statement of claim.

Refer to Forecast - penalty and interest
Opens in a new window
 where you need to determine the amount of penalty or interest incurred since the last system update, but update of the account is not necessary. For example, to advise a taxpayer over the phone of the amount outstanding on an account up to a particular date.

Suppression or accounting treatment present

Where the update of penalty and interest includes GIC, and the account is subject to a No Auto GIC accounting treatment, Debit processing or All suppression type, GIC is calculated but is recorded as Notional GIC, using non-financial transactions. This is to indicate the amount of GIC that would be physically applied to the account if the accounting treatment or suppression were not active. Notional GIC is not included in the account balance and is not a collectable debt.

If an update of GIC is initiated and a No Auto GIC accounting treatment, Debit processing or All suppression type is active for the account, a Manual GIC update review item is generated. This is to:

initiate a manual review of the account, and
if necessary, a manual update of GIC.

Where the update penalty and interest user interface is used, a success message will be displayed even if notional GIC has been applied and a review item created.

If an update of GIC is initiated requiring an amendment to existing GIC that includes a manual GIC transaction, a Manual GIC auto period review item is generated. In these situations manual calculation of GIC and additional manual GIC transactions to update the account are required. These review items will stream according to rules specified in Siebel work distribution.

First Home Saver Account (FHSA) manual penalties should not be used at this point. Government FHSA contributions ceased on 30 June 2017. No new FHSA roles can be created.

