Update - Correct over remission of penalty cost or fine

Correct over remission of interest, penalty, cost or fines in ICP.

Last published 28 April 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 
Background

This page provides the procedure to correct an error that has resulted in an over remission of interest, penalty, cost or fines in the ICP system.

Overview

Various types of interest, penalties, costs and fines are imposed and remitted in ICP.

Generally when processing a remission in ICP, the remission amount field defaults to the full amount of interest, penalty, cost or fine available to be remitted. The remission posted may be more than you intended where:

this amount is not modified prior to saving the remission, or
the amount typed in this field is incorrect.

Warning: Do not remit the amount of GIC subject to an (uneconomical to pursue) non-pursuit without re-raising
Opens in a new window
 the debt. These types of non-pursuit must be re-raised:

if you consider remission of that GIC is appropriate in the specific circumstances of the case, or
to absorb credits that may arise after a remission.

If you are unsure, seek assistance from your coach.

ICP was not designed to allow remission decisions to be changed once they have been processed. This is because, generally once a remission decision is made, it cannot legally be reversed. Whether you can correct an 'accidental' remission will depend on the circumstances of your case.

Follow this procedure if you have:

not notified the taxpayer of your decision, or
remitted an amount that is not legally available to be remitted (e.g. you have remitted an amount that has already been released), or
made an error inputting the remission amount when processing your remission decision you can follow this procedure.

If you have notified the taxpayer of your remission decision, seek help from Debt support staff.

