Remit/refuse GIC

Case officers in SR&I can be requested to remit GIC for taxpayers from other areas such as Litigation and Legal Services. On occasions, they provide an executed Deed of Settlement. The deed:

outlines the GIC amount to be remitted. These amounts can be beyond the authorisation limit of the case officer actioning the request
is deemed as written authorisation to remit the GIC. Therefore, the case officer is not required to seek further approval for amounts beyond their authorisation limit.

No authorisations are required for the imposition of GIC because they are imposed by operation of law.

If the amount of GIC requested for remission across both accounts is above your authorisation, the entire request must be escalated to the appropriate level. For example, you must not:

remit GIC on the income tax account for an amount within your authorisation, and

escalate the remainder on the Integrated Client Account (ICA) for an amount above your authorisation.

